## 6. High-level design

```mermaid
%%{init: {"themeVariables": {"fontSize": "20px"}, "flowchart": {"nodeSpacing": 80, "rankSpacing": 90, "padding": 24}}}%%
flowchart TB
  User([User / UI])
  API["API<br/>Express + TypeScript"]
  DB[("Database<br/>PostgreSQL")]
  TQ[["Task queue<br/>RabbitMQ"]]
  W["Workers × W<br/>Python"]
  FS[("File storage<br/>shared folder")]
  RQ[["Result queue<br/>RabbitMQ"]]
  S["Scheduler<br/>TypeScript"]

  User -->|1. create job| API
  API -->|2. save job| DB
  API -->|3. first tasks| TQ
  TQ -->|4. one task at a time| W
  W <-->|5. read and write files| FS
  W -->|6. task done| RQ
  RQ --> S
  S -->|7. next tasks| TQ
  S -->|record progress| DB
```

**How it works:**

1. The user creates a job through the **API**.
2. The API saves the job in the **database**.
3. The API puts the first tasks (generate the files) on the **task queue**.
4. Each free **worker** takes one task.
5. The worker reads its inputs from **file storage**, does the work, and saves the output there.
6. The worker reports "done, here's the count" on the **result queue**.
7. The **scheduler** adds the result to the job's waiting items. When 5 are ready, it creates the next combine task. When everything left fits in one task and nothing else is running, it creates the **final task**, which also divides each position's sum by the count. When the final task finishes, the job is done.

Steps 4–7 repeat until the job finishes.

The **scheduler** is a module of the TypeScript backend (same codebase as the API), not an extra system. The bonus **worker manager** (not shown) starts or stops worker processes to match W.

