## 8. Key design decisions

**Combining results.** Each task returns a **list of per-position sums plus a count**, never averages. Averaging averages is wrong when groups have different sizes, and the 5-file limit guarantees they will. Lists are added position by position (index 0 with index 0, and so on), counts are added together, and this works in any order. This is the same principle federated learning uses to combine results from different participants: each result is weighted by how much data it came from.

**Splitting a job.** A job with F > 5 becomes many queue items: ⌈F / 5⌉ generate tasks plus about (F − 1) / 4 combine tasks, because each 5-input combine turns 5 items into 1. No task ever has more than 5 inputs.

**Enforcing the 5-file limit (simulated RAM).** The limit is guaranteed in three places: the scheduler never builds a task with more than 5 inputs; each worker takes only one task at a time (`prefetch = 1`) and rejects any task with more than 5 inputs; and a worker loads one input at a time while adding. Optionally, each worker process also gets a real operating-system memory cap.

**Distributing work.** Workers **pull one task at a time** from the queue, so fast workers naturally do more. Results are combined **as soon as up to 5 are ready**, from any level of the tree, so nobody waits for a "round" to finish. Near the end of a job, an idle worker can run a **backup copy** of a task stuck on a slow worker; the first result wins.

**Knowing a job is finished.** The final task is created only when all remaining items are in it, nothing else is running, and nothing else is being generated. Afterwards the system checks that the final count equals **F**.

**Multiple jobs.** The queue is kept short and refilled by **taking turns between jobs**, so a large job can't block a small one.

